-- 435 places were named after a road number.
--
-- The OSM import names an unnamed object after the road it sits on
-- (osm-import.mjs:905, `road ?? area ?? town`), and for these rows the road was
-- a route designation rather than a street: "I 568", "AC 791", "BD 805". Five
-- separate playgrounds came out called "E 4" — a motorway that runs the length
-- of the country, so the name located nothing at all.
--
-- The fix is the second branch of that same expression, which the street beat:
-- the suburb. Reverse-geocoded one row at a time at Nominatim's one request per
-- second, 412 of the 435 returned a real suburb, village or hamlet; the other 23
-- had only a municipality, run through the same ungenitive() the importer uses
-- so "Nyköpings kommun" lands as "Nyköping" and not "Nyköpings" — the trap that
-- migrations 0133-0136 had to clean up after.
--
-- The category noun is appended because the suburb alone would not say what the
-- place is: "Morön" becomes "Morön Skjutbana". Nouns are singular and Swedish,
-- which neither the categories table (English) nor CATEGORY_NAMES (plural, and
-- sometimes an activity rather than a place — "Basket") could supply.
--
-- Ordinary street-named places are deliberately NOT touched. The page title
-- already carries category and city — "Nydalastigen — Basketball in Malmö" —
-- so appending a noun there buys nothing and says it twice.
--
-- Two rows still share a name: Rögle Hundrastgård, Lund. Their coordinates are
-- 100 m apart, so they are two dog runs rather than one row twice. They shared
-- the name "M 945" before this migration as well.
--
-- Old names are listed here rather than stored in a column: this file is the
-- record, and reverting is a sed away from the same VALUES list.

